These terms are an agreement between you and Dmitrii Emelianov PR Računarsko programiranje Novi Sad (Dmitrii Emelianov), a sole proprietorship registered in Serbia (“we”, “us”), the developer of Hoptail: Novi Sad, Serbia, registered with the Serbian Business Registers Agency (APR), registration number (matični broj) 67225627, tax ID (PIB) 113970406.

They cover the beta of Hoptail: the Hoptail iPhone app, the `hoptail` program for macOS with its Homebrew formula, our push notification relay and the documentation that comes with them (together, “Hoptail”). By installing or using Hoptail you agree to these terms. If you don’t agree, don’t install it, or uninstall it.

You get the iPhone app through Apple’s TestFlight, so Apple’s TestFlight terms apply to it too. These terms are between you and us: Apple isn’t a party to them and isn’t responsible for Hoptail.

## 1. What Hoptail is

Hoptail is a remote control for your own tools.

- The `hoptail` program runs on your Mac, in your user account. It shows your iPhone what your tmux sessions and coding agents (such as Claude Code) are doing, and passes on what you send from your iPhone: your answers, your Allow or Deny, your text and keystrokes.
- Everything that happens on your computer is done by your own programs and agents, on your commands and with the permissions you give them. Hoptail doesn’t run code on your projects itself and doesn’t approve anything on its own.
- We don’t store your code, terminal output, messages or the content of notifications. They go between your Mac and your iPhone; notifications pass through our relay end-to-end encrypted, so the relay can’t read them. What the relay does store, such as an encrypted push token and an opaque handle, is described in the Hoptail Privacy Policy: https://hoptail.app/privacy.

## 2. What you may do

While the beta lasts, we give you a free, personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable and revocable license to install and use the iPhone app and to install and run the `hoptail` program on Macs you own or control, to use them together with our relay, and to test Hoptail and tell us what you think.

## 3. What you may not do

You may not, and may not let anyone else:

- copy Hoptail except as needed to install and run it, or distribute, publish, sell, rent, lend, sublicense or share it. Point others to TestFlight and the Homebrew tap instead;
- decompile, disassemble, reverse engineer or extract the program code of Hoptail, or try to get at its source code, except where applicable law expressly allows this despite this restriction;
- modify Hoptail, remove or change its signature, notices or labels, or get around its technical limits;
- use the relay other than through Hoptail, overload it, or try to read or disrupt other people’s notifications;
- use Hoptail to break the law or the rights of others, or to get access to computers or accounts you aren’t authorized to use.

## 4. It’s a beta

Hoptail is pre-release. It may have bugs, change or lose features, stop working with older versions of the app or the `hoptail` program, or be withdrawn at any time, with or without notice.

## 5. Your responsibility

- **What you approve is your decision.** You’re responsible for what you send through Hoptail, including every Allow, answer, command and keystroke, and for what your agents and programs then do on your computers, servers, websites, production systems, accounts and data.
- **Look before you allow.** A notification or a screen on your iPhone shows only part of what an agent is about to do. If you’re not sure, open the terminal or answer at your Mac.
- **Keep backups.** Use version control and backups for anything that matters. Restoring your systems and data is up to you.
- **Not for critical systems.** Don’t use Hoptail to control systems whose failure could lead to death, personal injury, or serious damage to property or the environment, or other systems where downtime is critical.
- **Keep your devices safe.** Anyone who can use your unlocked iPhone with Hoptail paired can send input to your Mac. Lock your iPhone, and unpair a device you’ve lost.

## 6. Other companies’ services

Hoptail works with tools and services we don’t make or control: Claude Code and other coding agents, tmux, Tailscale, Homebrew, and Apple’s TestFlight and push notification service. They run under their own terms and your own accounts with their providers. We’re not responsible for their availability, changes or failures, or for what they do.

